# shellcheck shell=bash
# shellcheck disable=SC2034 # these are read by the scripts that source this file
#
# The live-network smoke target, committed so the smoke test is not decorative.
#
# `scripts/test-testnet.sh` and `.github/workflows/testnet.yml` both read this file, so
# there is one place that says which contract the scheduled test analyses. Before this
# existed the workflow needed a repository variable that nobody had set, so the job
# printed a notice and exited successfully - a green run that had checked nothing.
#
# The contract is a Soroban market on testnet. It was chosen for a property, not for
# convenience: it both *is* called and *makes* calls to another contract, in ordinary
# trading activity. That is the smallest real case that exercises the whole chain the
# engine claims to support - observation, cross-contract call recovery, dependency
# classification, evidence, confidence and impact - so a run against it can assert
# something about the flagship analysis rather than only that the CLI exited zero.
#
# It is not an endorsement and nothing about the analysis is a claim that the contract
# is secure, safe or correct. Amasario is not a security scanner.
#
# Which contract is named here is a maintenance decision, not a correctness one: a
# contract that stops being called will make the smoke test fail, and the fix is to name
# a busier one. That failure is the test working - it means the world the target was
# chosen from has changed, which is one of the things a live test is for.
AMASARIO_TARGET_NETWORK=testnet
AMASARIO_TARGET_CONTRACT=CAYPAQDKNWMHRATKU5DQ327VDHVRSIVK7UGVWT2A5SUZCUFTLUHXH2JA
